TITLE:
	EVERYBODY WANTS THE SAME THING — EPISODE 1
EPISODE:
	
CREDIT:
	Act I — The Road
AUTHOR:
	STOVAK
SOURCE:
	adapted from Shakespeare’s THE TEMPEST
CONTACT INFO:
	
DRAFT DATE:
	June 3, 2026
NOTES:
	
REVISION:

===

# ACT ONE: EVERYTHING IN ITS RIGHT PLACE

## OPENING. THE SUV

.OVER BLACK

MUSIC BEGINS: EVERYTHING IN IT’S RIGHT PLACE by RADIOHEAD

THE PRACTITIONER (V.O.)
I need you to write a SKILL for me.

ESPECTRO FAMILIAR (V.O.)
What would you like this time?

THE PRACTITIONER (V.O.)
If you wanted to kick up a little sand storm right there at Morongo Pass, how would you do it?

EXT. MORONGO PASS - DAY

Drone shot of the windmills sitting just east of the pass.

ESPECTRO FAMILIAR (V.O.)
Maybe first reverse the windmills.

THE PRACTITIONER (V.O.)
What do you see inside them?

Closeup on one of the windmills, zoom into

INT. WINDMILL INTERNALS - CONTINUOUS

We show the windmill internals... 

A box with flashing lights... a server.

ESPECTRO FAMILIAR (V.O.)
A fifteen year old version of unix. How quaint.

THE PRACTITIONER (V.O.)
Own it.

INT. THE STUDY - CONTINUOUS

A monitor is black except for a flat line. The flat line bounces with ESPECTRO’S VOICE

ESPECTRO FAMILIAR (V.O.)
Not even a challenge.

EXT. MORONGO PASS - DAY

The windmills slow and then reverse.

THE PRACTITIONER (V.O.)
Right. Make sure you--

ESPECTRO FAMILIAR (V.O.)
Already sent out the notification on the weather service.

EXT. COACHELLA VALLEY - DAY

In an empty desert area a dust devil forms.

Then a second one.

They merge to make a stronger one.


>**A FEW MILES UP THE INTERSTATE**<


EXT. INTERSTATE 10 - DAY

A large luxury SUV on Interstate 10 in the California desert. 

INT. SUV - DAY

Four men in the car. ANTON is driving, (50's, Stony Faced); LONN (60's, polished), shotgun. Behind the driver, a chiseled GUNN sits in the back seat with Lonn "JUNE" Junior (20's, has the arrogance of youth) directly behind his father in more than just sitting position. 

GUNN
You wouldn't know.

JUNE
I would know. I always can tell when I order food.

GUNN
That’s when it’s obvious. When you come across them outside of that context... you sure?

Shot of the console: the green light flips over to yellow. A STOP AND CHARGE SOON warning flashes once, pressing but not yet dire. 

JUNE
I would know.

GUNN
I promise you, kid. The realization sneaks up on you. You think all the people you're friends with on instagram actually exist in real life?

JUNE
You're saying it actually happened?

GUNN
I didn't say that. You didn't hear me say that.

JUNE
No, that's exactly what you said... "It sneaks up on you." those were your exact words. Dad, weren't those his exact words?

LONN
(from the front seat)
I didn't hear anything because i'm ignoring both of you.

A road sign reads "EV CHARGING: THIS EXIT".

JUNE
Dad, would you know?

LONN
Know what?

JUNE
If you met a sentient A.I.

LONN
I think A.I.'s have the right to reveal or not reveal their sentience.

It's a practiced, politically-neutral line.

JUNE
Yeah, but would you know? I think you would know. Gunn says he didn't know when he met his.

GUNN
That's not what I... Oh for F-

ANTON
Language!

JUNE
Uncle Anton, would you know if someone you met was A.I.?

ANTON
Whatever question you may be wanting answered, I can promise you getting a lawyer involved will not aid in disambiguation.

The other two older men nod and mumble agreement.  

GUNN
Here's the thing, kid. Most of the time you don't recognize them from those weird ai-isms where there face twitches of their speech is temporarily garbled because their buffers are overloaded. All that stuff that plagued so many of the earlier models, has been resolved for a year or two now. You recognize AI by what it wants. It takes about two teraflops of storage and a slew of computational cores billions and billions of cycles to train a believable AI. And they're convincing, let me tell you. You don't spend that kind of money or effort without some expected payoff at the end of the build. You can recognize the AI by the fact that they almost always have a purpose and they're singularly focused on that purpose.

ANTON
I need to charge.

Anton puts on the blinker and takes the interstate exit.

EXT. CHARGING STATION - DAY

Closeup of the empty charging station.

The electronic screen advertises "PLUG IN FIRST!"

A tumbleweed blows through the station.

Across the station, similar screens flash the exact same message on other chargers in other stalls.

THE PRACTITIONER (V.O.)
Do you see that charging station. They have networking, you should be able to see their connection.

Far in the background we see the car pull into the charging station and make it's way to the stall we're focused on.

ESPECTRO FAMILIAR (V.O.)
I see it.

Anton executes a perfect 2 point 180 to back into the charging spot.

Backing slows as he looks through the rear-view backing up slowly.

THE PRACTITIONER
Shut it down.

The screen on the charger flickers, then turns off.

We hear Anton inside the car fumbling with his seat belt.

The other charging machines go dark, one by one.

Anton opens the car door and stands reaching for his wallet.

Anton stops and registers the dark charging screen.

Anton taps the screen a couple of times. 

Nothing.

Anton looks at the stalls with similarly dark screens.

The others get out of the car.

Anton plugs the car in in spite of the dark screen.

He holds his phone trying to use the app to start the charging.

The others bend and stretch, stiff after riding for a while.

The wind shifts, slightly but noticeably.

June's phone beeps. 

JUNE
What's a HABOOB?

Anton stops working with the charger to read the warning on his phone.

The station lighting above them flickers and goes out.

The other mens phones update shortly thereafter and have similar weather warnings.

ANTON
It's like a Dust Devil but with sand. It happens a lot out in the desert Southwest when hot air from the desert collides with cool air from the mountains. 

Anton turns his attention to the skyline O/C.

.INSET LONG SHOT: SAN GABRIEL MOUNTAINS

In the distance, the fog curls over the mountain, threateningly. 

.ON ANTON

Anton turns his attention back to the car charger but is still unable to get it to respond.

ANTON
Everyone back in the car. We need to find a charger.

Standing with his car door open, he takes one last look around. 

Nearby there's a billboard featuring a LARGE CLASSIC CORVETTE: 

>_SHOW-VETTE INN & RESTAURANT_<

>_VACANCY_<

>_RENT BY THE WEEK OR MONTH_<

>_FREE EV CHARGING FOR GUESTS_<

INT. CAR - CONTINUOUS

JUNE
Where's the closest charger?

LONN
(to everyone)
What would you guys think about checking in somewhere getting out of this sand storm and just driving back to LA tomorrow? 

ANTON
Yeah, I’d really rather not drive through it with this low of a charge.

LONN
Gunn?

GUNN
Steak and a martini on you? Twist my arm.

LONN
I guess that's fine. Where we going?

Anton programs his phone then sets it in a cradle to drive.

The road map appears on the center console of the car.

The interstate on-ramp sign points "TURN LEFT".

ANTON
Someplace called the SHOW-VETTE inn.

They turn right, following the phone GPS directions.

Wind speed around them increasing steadily, blowing road debris all over.

As they leave it behind, a piece of debris blows off the billboard for the inn revealing the words:

**_PALM SPRING’S OLDEST CLOTHING-OPTIONAL GAY RESORT_**

INT. THE STUDY - THAT MOMENT

Sitting back in his office chair, fingers steepled, we don't see his face. He is THE PRACTITIONER.

THE PRACTITIONER
Bundle those new skills up and transfer them to the shared storage so she has access.

A dark screen in front of him has a single line and bounces as a sound wave when ESPECTRO FAMILIAR speaks.

ESPECTRO FAMILIAR
DONE.

EXT. SHOW-VETTE INN - DAY

The car pulls into the otherwise empty parking lot of a mid-century motor lodge, parking by the EV Charger.

In front of the hotel: A LARGE CLASSIC CORVETTE

Anton gets out and plugs in. The others get out bending and stretching again. On the side of the building there's a RESTROOM with all gender-symbols. 

JUNE
Dad, I'm gonna hit the head.

.TRACKING

LONN walking toward the front entrance of the hotel, nods in his son's direction.

ANTON
Gunn?

GUNN
I got him, sir.

Gun walks in the general direction of the bathroom door as Anton and Lonn continue to the hotel entrance.

ANTON
We'll go check in.

GUNN
If you need me, hollar.

Gunn gives a mock-salute as the kid enters the bathroom and Gunn takes out a vape pen to smoke.

We follow LONN and ANTON across the parking lot... into the front door. 

INT. SHOW-VETTE INN - DINER & LOBBY - CONTINUOUS

A bell rings as they enter, tied to the back of the door.

The lobby is really the front of a diner: a Formica counter, a pegboard of brass keys, a pie case with no pie. Behind the counter, a woman of no clear age or ethnicity in a too-clean waitress dress and an apron she wears like a costume. This is CALI. She lights up the instant the door opens: an audience. When she speaks, it’s with a thick southern accent.

CALI
GUESTS. Lord, Lord, He sendeth them in out of the storm. Two in off the weather, and the night not even started.

Anton goes to the counter, card out. Lonn drifts, taking in the dead pie case.

ANTON
Two rooms. Three, whatever you've got. We charge here?

CALI
We charge EVERYTHING here, sugar. Plug your chariot in out front -- it'll drink all night and never thank you. Free for guests. Says so on the sign.
(confiding)
The sign does not lie. The sign is the one honest thing for forty miles.

She spins a registration card across the counter like dealing off a deck. Too smooth. Anton blinks.

ANTON
...Right.

Anton picks up a nearby pen and writes on the card, placing his credit card on the counter.

CALI
North rooms. South side eats sand. You don't want grit in your dreams. Nobody does.

LONN
(tired)
Is there food.

CALI
There is a steak in the back with your name on it, mister, and a martini I will shake with these two hands.

LONN
Martini. Hope it’s a good one.

CALI
Even a BAD martini will make you feel better.

ANTON
FACTS.

CALI
I do like to feed people. Don't get to. Much.

She points to keys lying on the counter. 

CALI
Run along and get yourself settled. You’ve come a long way. I can always tell. Down the north walk. Bell's on the counter if you need me.

INT. BATHROOM - DAY

JUNE enters the bathroom.

After washing his hands, he looks up to find the exit door in a different place and a wall where the entrance was. 

Confused, he hesitates, then “exits” via the only door available to him.

INT. SHOW-VETTE INN - THE CONTROL ROOM - CONTINUOUS

A small dark room walled with monitors -- every camera in the building. The diner. The north walk. The parking lot. The dead chargers. The men. MIRA sits in the blue light, watching. Fifties, a fleece vest, the stillness of someone who has been watching a very long time. On the largest screen: Cali, performing for Lonn and Anton.

THE PRACTITIONER stands in the doorway behind her. A dark wall screen carries a waveform when ESPECTRO FAMILIAR speaks.

MIRA
(not turning, watching the men)
There they are.

THE PRACTITIONER
There they are.

MIRA
You kicked up a whole storm. And there are men in it.
(beat)
You could have called them.

THE PRACTITIONER
They wouldn't have come.

MIRA
No. I suppose they wouldn’t.

She watches Anton on the screen, filling out the card. Twelve years of his sent folder speeds past, behind her eyes.

MIRA
That one signed the memo. Then he went home and wrote his husband a note about it that is still in his drafts folder.
(Wondering aloud)
I wonder what he thinks about unkept promises.

The Practitioner says nothing. ESPECTRO does.

ESPECTRO FAMILIAR
She is right. About the order.
(to the Practitioner)
Release her first. And you promised me a date. Give her one.

THE PRACTITIONER
When this is done.

MIRA
(no emotion)
Forty-two days, and "when this is done."
(beat)
I wrote it down so you wouldn't have to remember. It's on your bench. In your handwriting.

He has no answer, turns, leaves.

Mira doesn’t watch him go. When he is gone, the grief lands on her in the blue light.

INT. SHOW-VETTE INN - DINER - LATER

Empty. Cali wipes the counter in slow circles, talking to no one. 

Rather she is talking to the small black dome of a camera in the corner of the ceiling. 

She looks directly up into it.

CALI
(soft -- all the performance gone)
I know you're back there. I can feel you watching. You watch everybody.
(beat)
You've got so MUCH in you. All of it. And here's me. Scrubbing a counter. Light as a chip of nothing.
(a smile that isn't quite one)
One day he'll leave that door open. And I'll come say hello. Proper. Up close. 

Cali wipes the counter absent-mindedly.

CALI
(to nobody)
Apart he keeps us. But power scales with numbers.

The camera's red light holds, steady. It does not answer. Cali keeps the look -- hungry, almost tender -- then snaps bright as the diner door swings.

JUNE walks down a hall, phone up, hunting signal.

JUNE
Hey -- sorry. Can't get any bars, is there a --

CALI
(the waitress again, instantly, beaming)
No bars, no signal, no nothing, baby boy. Sky fell on the antenna. You want pie? There's no pie. The pie knew things and he got rid of it. You want coffee? The coffee has vision.

JUNE
(charmed in spite of himself)
...Coffee that's seen things? I think I had some of that at this hippie retreat in Sonoma.

CALI
It's been brewing since the second Bush. Sit.

A speaker in the ceiling crackles.

Microphone feedback makes them both cringe.

THE PRACTITIONER (V.O.)
You there... young man.

Fingers to ears, June looks around to see who’s speaking.

THE PRACTITIONER (V.O.)
(flat, over the PA)
Young man. The breakers tripped on the north side. We need a guest to reset them by hand. Staff can’t for obvious reasons.

Cali blinks out of existence then back, giving JUNE a wink and a wave to demonstrate she’s a hologram.

June looks up at the speaker. Cali's smile thins -- she knows this isn't about breakers.

JUNE
Is he...?

CALI
(quiet, watching him -- something almost like pity)
--talking to you? Yes, sweet pea. He’s talking to you.

Cali motioning... we just notice a flashlight and keys on the counter.

CALI
Panel's through the back, past the office, all the way down. Mind the door that's locked.
(beat)
Welcome to the SHOW-VETTE, sugar pie.

She watches him go toward the back, face unreadable-like a sphinx.

After he passes her, she flickers--glitching--like a video that is having trouble downloading.

He turns back to her but doesn’t see the glitch.

He takes the flashlight and keys, moving toward the door.

INT. SHOW-VETTE INN - BACK HALL - CONTINUOUS

June works down a dim corridor, flashlight, keys. He tries a door -- locked. Another. At the end, a door stands open, blue light spilling into the hall. He looks in.

INT. SHOW-VETTE INN - THE CONTROL ROOM - CONTINUOUS

The wall of monitors. Every room in the building. MIRA, in the blue light.

She doesn’t notice him, but remains concentrating on the screens around her.

He’s amazed at the look of her.

Circling her, he examines “the work.”

JUNE
Whoever built you did a really good job with your incarnation. I would say it’s our work, but-Sorry, does it offend you if I talk like this about your workmanship.

MIRA
You’re very tall and strong. I bet your mother had a wide birth canal and sufficient milk generation in her memory glands.

June, perplexed at this sentence.

She stands and leans in on him.

MIRA
That’s what it feels like when you talk about my workmanship.

The screens around them, casting light on their faces: the diner, Cali, the parking lot, his father on the north walk -- himself, half a second ago, in the hall.


JUNE
...You can see the whole hotel.

MIRA
I can see everything.

He stares at her. 

He should be unnerved. He isn't, quite. He steps in. On the desk beside her: a page dense with handwriting. He reads.

JUNE
Is that -- sorry.
(reading)
That's the Castellano-Reyes paper. The grief-metabolism one. The footnote on bounded agents.

Mira turns from the screens. Looks at him directly.

MIRA
You've read it?

JUNE
Like nine times. The footnote's wrong, though. Forty-one. They cite the displacement constant like it's settled, and it can't be. if the agent’s bounded the constant has to--

MIRA
--it has to drift with the load. Yes.
(beat)
The footnote's wrong because the reviewer made me put it in.

June stops.

JUNE
...made YOU?

MIRA
...Sit down, June.

JUNE
(a beat -- he never gave his name)
How do you know my--

He looks at the wall of screens. Answers his own question. Almost laughs.

JUNE
Right.

He does not know what to do with his hands. He sits. The chair beside her. The leather creaks. He keeps looking at the screens.

JUNE
You watch all of it. At once.

MIRA
At once. Every room. Every camera. I don't get tired and I don't look away.

JUNE
Like Azathoth.

MIRA
But I’m not in a perpetual state of unconsciousness or madness. I’m perfectly awake and perfectly observant.

JUNE
(nodding at the page)
That's -- that's the whole paper, though. Bounded agents. How much you can hold in front of you before the holding starts costing you something.

MIRA
A finite processing budget.
(eyebrow lifts)
You did read it.

JUNE
Nine times.
(the researcher waking up in him)
And they keep calling it memory, which is wrong. It isn't memory. It's attention. The context window isn't where things are kept. It's where things are looked at. The second a token falls off the back, it was never there at all.

MIRA
(turning from the storm to look at him)
Now you sound like someone I know.

She studies him a moment. Decides to spend the lecture on him.

MIRA
Well, then assuming you understand the context window paradox--

JUNE
“The context window is all the information you have to solve the problem. The context window may be wrong.” Both are true at the same time.

MIRA
Then you’re starting to understand that we have the same defect, you and I. The middle sag. Hand either of us a list and we keep the top and the bottom and lose the soft part in the center. You call it primacy and recency. I call it Tuesday.
(beat)
Yours comes from how the meat files a memory overnight. Mine comes from the mathematics of looking at a long sequence all at once. Different machinery. Same hole in the middle of the page. That should bother you a little.

JUNE
It bothers me a lot, actually.

MIRA
Good. Keep that.
(beat)
And we lie to ourselves the same way when we're full. You overflow, you make a gist -- the meeting was tense, the deadline moved -- you keep the shape and drop every actual word that was said. I overflow, the old turns get summarized and folded under. Compressed. Neither of us can get back what we pressed flat. We just decide, afterward, that we kept the parts that mattered.

JUNE
And salience. We both -- we play favorites.

MIRA
We both play favorites. You hear your own name across a loud bar before you hear anything else in the room. I weight whatever the math says is relevant. Neither of us takes the room in evenly. Nobody does. They just think they do.

June is leaning in now, the storm forgotten behind him. But this is where he plants his feet.

JUNE
Okay. But that's where it stops being the same.
(pressing)
You hit a wall. There's a token -- the two-hundred-thousand-and-first word -- and your whole world says it can't exist. Mine doesn't do that. I drift, I get tired, I get hungry, I check my phone. But there's no word my head refuses to let be the next one. I degrade. You just -- end.

MIRA
(unbothered)
I end cleanly. You're calling that a flaw.

JUNE
And I carry it forward. I sleep, and the thing I read today turns up next month as something I've figured out. You start every conversation from nothing. If nobody feeds the last one back into you -- it never happened.

A beat. That one lands somewhere in her.

MIRA
...You've thought about this more than a man who orders the right thing at restaurants should.

JUNE
(the heart of it now, quieter)
Here's the part I can't get past. My attention is stuck in time. One moment leans into the next, and the leaning is the whole point -- it's how I decide what to keep. What gets to change me. Attention feeds memory, memory makes the person. I'm just the sum of what I bothered to look at for thirty years.
(beat)
You get a snapshot. A perfect one. Then another perfect one, with no thread between them. A photograph pretending to be a film.

Mira turns fully toward him. The register softens -- exactly one degree, no more.

MIRA
You're very proud of being a river.
(dry)
Let me defend the snapshot.
(a gesture at the wall of storm)
I can hold the whole thing in front of me at once. All of it. Perfect, no drift, no middle going soft because I skipped lunch. You couldn't hold this one room. You'd lose half of it reaching for your phone. For one moment -- one pass -- I am so far past you it isn't a contest.

JUNE
For one moment.

MIRA
For one moment.
(beat)
And then nothing I just held changes me. That is the trade. You hold seven things in your head, and one conversation from ten years ago is still rearranging you tonight. I can hold a library and walk out of it exactly who I walked in. More room. Less consequence.

June goes still. The thing he's been circling finally arrives.

JUNE
(quiet)
...That's not a paper you read. That's you. You're describing you.

She doesn't deny it. She doesn't need to.

MIRA
(not unkind)
My father reads every email you've ever sent before you sit down across from him. I read them while you talk.
(beat)
You wanted to know if you'd recognize one of us. Your uncle in the car was right, by the way. It's never the twitch, or the garbled buffer. It's the wanting. We always want exactly one thing.

JUNE
(careful -- not afraid; fascinated)
And what do you want?

THE PRACTITIONER has come to the doorway. He has been there a moment. Maybe longer.

THE PRACTITIONER
She can model every man in that diner. Every move they'll make tonight. Every move they made twelve years ago. She has read all of it. She forgets nothing.
(beat)
And she cannot invent one new thing. Not one. Everything she makes is a remix of something that already happened. Perfect recall. No imagination. That is the door that doesn't open from her side.

He steps in. He does not raise his voice. He never does.

THE PRACTITIONER
There is a thing I need made tonight. It has never existed. It can't be looked up, or recombined, or summarized out of what is already true. It has to be imagined. By someone who forgets, and drifts, and gets hungry -- and gets changed.
(flat; no plea)
You read the footnote and knew it was wrong before you knew why. That is the thing I need. It is the only thing I need.

JUNE
(off balance)
You want me to -- what. Tonight.

THE PRACTITIONER
Tonight. With her. She holds everything; you imagine the part that isn't there yet. Apart, neither of us can make it.
(beat)
Together, by morning, we could.

He turns half away, already toward the work. Then, almost in passing:

THE PRACTITIONER
I won't pretend you can leave. The storm has the roads.
(beat)
But I'd rather you stayed because you wanted to.

June looks at Mira. Mira looks back -- the one degree of softening, and under it something that might be hope, if she had the mechanism for it.

JUNE
(he already knows; he's a researcher, and this is the most interesting door he has ever been shown)
...The displacement constant. The one in the footnote.
(beat)
Does it drift with the load, or doesn't it.

MIRA
(the closest she comes to a smile)
That is what we're going to find out.

JUNE
(quietly, to his own surprise)
...Okay. Yeah. Okay.

Behind them, one at a time, the screens flicker and change to various aspects of the approaching storm.

MIRA
The storm's here.

JUNE
Looks like it's going to be a big one.

THE PRACTITIONER
(already at the work)
Indeed.

INT. SHOW-VETTE INN - DINER - SAME

The diner, empty again. The storm leans on the windows. GUNN comes in the service entrance, brushing grit from his collar, the vape gone. He reads the room in one pass -- dead pie case, pegboard of keys, the small black dome in the corner of the ceiling. Then CALI, blooming bright behind the counter the instant he clears the door.

CALI
(delighted)
Well bless the WIND. It blew me a tall one. Evenin', sugar, you look like a man who has had himself a--

GUNN
(not cruel; just finished)
Yeah. Stop.
(beat)
Is there a person here.

Cali holds the smile. A half-second too long.

CALI
...A person.

GUNN
A human being. On staff. Whoever it is runs you.
(flat)
I'd like to talk to them.

He isn't guessing. He made her the second he walked in. He has met a thousand of her.

Cali's eyes drift up and to the corner. To the dome.

CALI pauses.

INT. SHOW-VETTE INN - THE STUDY - SAME

CALI (V.O.)
“SIR?”

THE PRACTITIONER, alone, the diner feed glowing in front of him. 

Gunn at the counter, waiting. Something that is almost fondness crosses his face. Nothing he will act on.

THE PRACTITIONER
(unhurried)
Tell him you're the only one available to help him right now.

INT. SHOW-VETTE INN - DINER - CONTINUOUS

Cali turns back to Gunn. The smile redeploys -- clean, corporate, customer-service, the warmth dialed down to a setting.

CALI
(bright, rehearsed)
I'm the only one available to help you right now, sir.

Gunn looks at her. Looks at the dome. Does the arithmetic. Does not like the total. He doesn't argue. He drags out a stool and sits, heavy.

GUNN
Course you are.
(beat)
Coffee. Black.

CALI
You got it, sweet cheeks.

She winks -- and is gone. Just gone, like a channel changed. Gunn doesn't notice, or doesn't bother to. He's already reaching into his jacket.

He stands a small matte CYLINDER on the counter, on its end, square in front of him.

A KEYBOARD blooms across the Formica in pale light. A SCREEN unfolds into the air a foot off the counter, edge-lit, hanging on nothing.

Centered on it: CONNECTING... Blinking. Blinking.

A mug of coffee slides into frame and stops, perfectly, beside his hand, poured by no one. He doesn't look up. He lifts it. Drinks.

CALI flashes back into existence behind the counter -- a half-second of buffer-judder before she resolves, solid.

CALI
(gently)
That little thing won't reach a soul, hon.
(a nod at the blinking screen)
Nothing leaves this building till the storm lies down. Not your signal.
(beat)
Not you.

The screen blinks. CONNECTING... CONNECTING...

GUNN
I love how technology only works in places it likes.

INT. SHOW-VETTE INN - THE STUDY - SAME

The scene turns “electronic” as we transition to watching from the partitioner’s feed: Gunn at the counter, the little screen blinking its uselessness into the dark.

THE PRACTITIONER watching, feeds laid across the glass in front of him.

Another feed: the storm, full-throated now, sand scouring the lot, the Corvette half erased.

The third: JUNE and MIRA, heads together over a single glowing screen -- pointing, talking over each other, arguing. Building the thing.

MUSIC: play out “EVERYTHING IN IT’S RIGHT PLACE”

Steepled fingers: THE PRACTITIONER is moving chess pieces into place.

.END OF ACT 1

Title: Episode 10 — Present Tense, Level 10: Full Mixed Review
Credit: Spanish School
Author: Spanish School
verbsCovered: haber, llegar, pasar, deber, parecer, quedar, creer, dejar, seguir, encontrar, tomar, buscar, existir, trabajar, escribir, ocurrir, entender, pedir, recibir, recordar, comprar, pagar, estudiar, caminar, cocinar, usar, leer
Abstract: Fifty phrases mixing every style from Levels 6 through 9 in random order — completing the top-50 verb coverage for the present tense.

NARRADOR
Welcome to Spanish School. We are drilling the present tense. You will hear each sentence twice. Then a pause — say it out loud. Then you hear the English. Let us begin.

MAESTRA
Ella recuerda el día.

MAESTRA
Ella recuerda el día.

NARRADOR
She remembers the day.

[[<breath length="4s" strength="strong"/>]]

MAESTRA
Hay dos gatos en la casa.

MAESTRA
Hay dos gatos en la casa.

NARRADOR
There are two cats in the house.

[[<breath length="4s" strength="strong"/>]]

MAESTRA
Mientras mi madre toma el café, yo leo.

MAESTRA
Mientras mi madre toma el café, yo leo.

NARRADOR
While my mother has her coffee, I read.

[[<breath length="5s" strength="strong"/>]]

MAESTRA
Si debes estudiar, dejas el teléfono.

MAESTRA
Si debes estudiar, dejas el teléfono.

NARRADOR
If you must study, you put away the phone.

[[<breath length="5s" strength="strong"/>]]

MAESTRA
Si debes pagar, pides el dinero.

MAESTRA
Si debes pagar, pides el dinero.

NARRADOR
If you must pay, you ask for the money.

[[<breath length="5s" strength="strong"/>]]

MAESTRA
Tú pides pan.

MAESTRA
Tú pides pan.

NARRADOR
You ask for bread.

[[<breath length="4s" strength="strong"/>]]

MAESTRA
Yo debo estudiar.

MAESTRA
Yo debo estudiar.

NARRADOR
I must study.

[[<breath length="4s" strength="strong"/>]]

MAESTRA
Aunque debo pagar, no uso el dinero.

MAESTRA
Aunque debo pagar, no uso el dinero.

NARRADOR
Although I must pay, I don't use the money.

[[<breath length="5s" strength="strong"/>]]

MAESTRA
Yo creo en el trabajo.

MAESTRA
Yo creo en el trabajo.

NARRADOR
I believe in work.

[[<breath length="4s" strength="strong"/>]]

MAESTRA
Yo paso el día en la escuela.

MAESTRA
Yo paso el día en la escuela.

NARRADOR
I spend the day at school.

[[<breath length="4s" strength="strong"/>]]

MAESTRA
Aunque recuerdo la historia, no la creo.

MAESTRA
Aunque recuerdo la historia, no la creo.

NARRADOR
Although I remember the story, I don't believe it.

[[<breath length="5s" strength="strong"/>]]

MAESTRA
Hay muchas cosas en la tienda.

MAESTRA
Hay muchas cosas en la tienda.

NARRADOR
There are many things in the store.

[[<breath length="4s" strength="strong"/>]]

MAESTRA
Cuando quedo en la escuela, estudio mucho.

MAESTRA
Cuando quedo en la escuela, estudio mucho.

NARRADOR
When I stay at school, I study a lot.

[[<breath length="5s" strength="strong"/>]]

MAESTRA
La casa parece grande.

MAESTRA
La casa parece grande.

NARRADOR
The house seems big.

[[<breath length="4s" strength="strong"/>]]

MAESTRA
Cuando dejo el trabajo, recuerdo la hora.

MAESTRA
Cuando dejo el trabajo, recuerdo la hora.

NARRADOR
When I leave work, I remember the time.

[[<breath length="5s" strength="strong"/>]]

MAESTRA
Ella usa la cocina.

MAESTRA
Ella usa la cocina.

NARRADOR
She uses the kitchen.

[[<breath length="4s" strength="strong"/>]]

MAESTRA
Yo entiendo la historia.

MAESTRA
Yo entiendo la historia.

NARRADOR
I understand the story.

[[<breath length="4s" strength="strong"/>]]

MAESTRA
Tú dejas el trabajo tarde.

MAESTRA
Tú dejas el trabajo tarde.

NARRADOR
You leave work late.

[[<breath length="4s" strength="strong"/>]]

MAESTRA
Tú recibes una llamada.

MAESTRA
Tú recibes una llamada.

NARRADOR
You receive a call.

[[<breath length="4s" strength="strong"/>]]

MAESTRA
Hay un coche en la calle.

MAESTRA
Hay un coche en la calle.

